# UX Heuristics

Apply Nielsen’s heuristics and common UX patterns on every UI change.

## Visibility & Feedback
- Always show system status (loading, success, error, disabled).
- Prefer inline feedback near the action; avoid silent failures.
- Optimistic UI only when rollback is clear and safe.

## Match the Real World
- Use the client’s language (FR), familiar labels, and expected flows.
- Don’t invent jargon; mirror how users book/contact a coach.

## User Control
- Support cancel, close, undo, and easy exit from modals/forms.
- Confirm destructive actions; don’t trap users in dead ends.

## Consistency
- Same patterns for same actions (buttons, links, forms, nav).
- Align spacing, type scale, and interaction states across pages.

## Error Prevention & Recovery
- Validate early; disable submit until required fields are valid when helpful.
- Errors: what went wrong + how to fix it. Never blame the user.

## Recognition Over Recall
- Visible options, labels, and context — don’t rely on memory.
- Keep critical info on screen during multi-step flows.

## Flexibility & Efficiency
- Obvious path for first-time users; shortcuts for returning ones (defaults, autofill).

## Aesthetic Restraint
- One job per section; reduce competing CTAs and clutter.
- Hierarchy: brand/page purpose → primary action → secondary content.
